---
import PlatformFrameworkBoundaryVisual from './PlatformFrameworkBoundaryVisual.astro';
import PrimitiveAbsorptionCycleVisual from './PrimitiveAbsorptionCycleVisual.astro';
import ResearchBreakout from './ResearchBreakout.astro';
import SignalStandardizationPathVisual from './SignalStandardizationPathVisual.astro';
---

<section class="overview not-prose" aria-labelledby="framework-platform-overview-title">
  <div class="overview__intro">
    <p class="overview__eyebrow">Overview · Conceptual version</p>
    <h2 id="framework-platform-overview-title">The browser is learning tricks frameworks used to handle themselves.</h2>
    <p class="overview__lead">
      React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, and other frameworks grew partly because the browser did not provide enough structure for building large applications. The browser platform is gradually gaining some of those missing pieces.
    </p>
    <p>
      This research asks a simple question: <strong>if more of those lower-level capabilities become native, what is still left for frameworks to do?</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="overview__answer" aria-label="Short answer">
    <p class="overview__eyebrow">The short answer</p>
    <p>
      Frameworks probably do not disappear. Instead, their job moves upward. The browser can take on more reusable mechanics, while frameworks keep making the opinionated decisions that turn those mechanics into an application.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ResearchBreakout mode="publication">
    <PlatformFrameworkBoundaryVisual />
  </ResearchBreakout>

  <div class="overview__explanation">
    <div>
      <p class="overview__eyebrow">What can move down</p>
      <h3>Reusable mechanics</h3>
      <p>
        Things such as component identity, navigation mechanics, transitions, common controls, and potentially reactive dependency tracking can become shared browser capabilities once the ecosystem understands them well enough.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="overview__eyebrow">What stays above</p>
      <h3>Application decisions</h3>
      <p>
        Frameworks still decide how work is scheduled, how application pieces compose, how routing and data loading fit together, how servers participate, and what development model a team uses.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="overview__section">
    <p class="overview__eyebrow">One example · Signals</p>
    <h3>The browser can standardize the plumbing without standardizing the whole house.</h3>
    <p>
      Several frameworks already use reactive graphs to track what depends on what. A shared Signals primitive could eventually give them common low-level plumbing. That would not force React, Vue, Angular, or another framework to schedule updates or render applications in the same way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ResearchBreakout mode="wide">
    <SignalStandardizationPathVisual />
  </ResearchBreakout>

  <div class="overview__section">
    <p class="overview__eyebrow">The larger pattern</p>
    <h3>Successful framework ideas can become platform building blocks.</h3>
    <p>
      The web has done this before. Once a useful mechanism becomes common, stable, and broadly understood, some of it can move into the platform. Frameworks can then stop rebuilding that mechanism and spend more of their energy coordinating the pieces above it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <ResearchBreakout mode="wide">
    <PrimitiveAbsorptionCycleVisual />
  </ResearchBreakout>

  <div class="overview__takeaways">
    <p class="overview__eyebrow">What I came away with</p>
    <div class="overview__takeaway-grid">
      <article>
        <span>01</span>
        <h3>The platform absorbs primitives.</h3>
        <p>Capabilities with broadly agreed semantics are good candidates to become native building blocks.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>02</span>
        <h3>Frameworks retain policy.</h3>
        <p>Scheduling, composition, server integration, conventions, and developer experience remain opinionated choices.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>03</span>
        <h3>The boundary moves, not vanishes.</h3>
        <p>The likely future is not “browser versus framework.” It is a different division of responsibility between them.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="overview__why">
    <p class="overview__eyebrow">Why this matters</p>
    <h3>This is really an architecture question.</h3>
    <p>
      If we can tell which abstractions are durable application policy and which exist mainly because the platform has not caught up yet, we can make better long-term frontend architecture decisions.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full research below examines the standards work, the limits of each primitive, and the responsibilities that still appear to belong above the platform layer.
    </p>

    <button class="overview__continue" type="button" data-reading-depth-open="full">
      <span>Continue to full research</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    </button>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .overview {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .overview__intro,
  .overview__section,
  .overview__why {
    max-width: 46rem;
  }

  .overview__intro h2 {
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin: 0.55rem 0 1.25rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
  }

  .overview p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .overview p + p {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .overview strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .overview__lead {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-size: 1.12rem !important;
    line-height: 1.75 !important;
  }

  .overview__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 !important;
    color: var(--accent) !important;
    font-family: var(--font-mono-ui);
    font-size: 0.625rem !important;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    line-height: 1.5 !important;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .overview__answer {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 4%, var(--bg-card));
    padding: 1.2rem 1.25rem;
  }

  .overview__answer p:last-child {
    max-width: 45rem;
    margin-top: 0.65rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.55;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .overview__explanation {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    margin: 2.75rem 0 3.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    background: var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .overview__explanation > div {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }

  .overview h3 {
    margin: 0.45rem 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.25;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .overview__section {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }

  .overview__takeaways {
    margin: 3.75rem 0;
  }

  .overview__takeaway-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    background: var(--border-subtle);
  }

  .overview__takeaway-grid article {
    background: var(--bg-card);
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }

  .overview__takeaway-grid article > span {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-mono-ui);
    font-size: 0.625rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
  }

  .overview__takeaway-grid h3 {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .overview__takeaway-grid p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  .overview__why {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    padding-top: 2rem;
  }

  .overview__continue {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.65rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0.2rem;
    background: transparent;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    font-family: var(--font-mono-ui);
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      background-color 160ms ease,
      color 160ms ease;
  }

  .overview__continue:hover {
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--bg-primary);
  }

  .overview__continue:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .overview__explanation {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .overview__takeaway-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .overview__continue {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
